            "output": "<path>/Users/aasheesh/Documents/webdev/os/cli/README.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Entire CLI\n2: \n3: Entire hooks into your Git workflow to capture AI agent sessions as you work. Sessions are indexed alongside commits, creating a searchable record of how code was written in your repo.\n4: \n5: With Entire, you can:\n6: \n7: - **Understand why code changed** — see the full prompt/response transcript and files touched\n8: - **Recover instantly** — rewind to a known-good checkpoint when an agent goes sideways and resume seamlessly\n9: - **Keep Git history clean** — preserve agent context on a separate branch\n10: - **Onboard faster** — show the path from prompt → change → commit\n11: - **Maintain traceability** — support audit and compliance requirements when needed\n12: \n13: ## Why Entire\n14: \n15: - **Understand why code changed, not just what** — Transcripts, prompts, files touched, token usage, tool calls, and more are captured alongside every commit.\n16: - **Rewind and resume from any checkpoint** — Go back to any previous agent session and pick up exactly where you or a coworker left off.\n17: - **Full context preserved and searchable** — A versioned record of every AI interaction tied to your git history, with nothing lost.\n18: - **Zero context switching** — Git-native, two-step setup, works with Claude Code, Codex, Gemini, and more.\n19: \n20: ## Table of Contents\n21: \n22: - [Why Entire](#why-entire)\n23: - [Quick Start](#quick-start)\n24: - [Typical Workflow](#typical-workflow)\n25: - [Key Concepts](#key-concepts)\n26:   - [How It Works](#how-it-works)\n27:   - [Strategy](#strategy)\n28: - [Local Device Auth Testing](#local-device-auth-testing)\n29: - [Commands Reference](#commands-reference)\n30: - [Configuration](#configuration)\n31: - [Security & Privacy](#security--privacy)\n32: - [Troubleshooting](#troubleshooting)\n33: - [Development](#development)\n34: - [Getting Help](#getting-help)\n35: - [License](#license)\n36: \n37: ## Requirements\n38: \n39: - Git\n40: - macOS, Linux or Windows\n41: - [Supported agent](#agent-hook-configuration) installed and authenticated\n42: \n43: ## Quick Start\n44: \n45: ```bash\n46: # Install stable via Homebrew\n47: brew tap entireio/tap\n48: brew install --cask entire\n49: \n50: # Or install nightly via Homebrew\n51: brew tap entireio/tap\n52: brew install --cask entire@nightly\n53: \n54: # Or install stable via install.sh\n55: curl -fsSL https://entire.io/install.sh | bash\n56: \n57: # Or install nightly via install.sh\n58: curl -fsSL https://entire.io/install.sh | bash -s -- --channel nightly\n59: \n60: # Or install stable via Scoop (Windows)\n61: scoop bucket add entire https://github.com/entireio/scoop-bucket.git\n62: scoop install entire/cli\n63: \n64: # Or install via Go (development/manual setup)\n65: go install github.com/entireio/cli/cmd/entire@latest\n66: \n67: # Linux: Add Go binaries to PATH (add to ~/.zshrc or ~/.bashrc if not already configured)\n68: export PATH=\"$HOME/go/bin:$PATH\"\n69: \n70: # Enable in your project\n71: cd your-project && entire enable\n72: \n73: # Check status\n74: entire status\n75: ```\n76: \n77: After the initial setup, use `entire agent` to add or remove agents, `entire configure` to update non-agent settings, and `entire enable` / `entire disable` to toggle Entire on or off.\n78: \n79: ## Release Channels\n80: \n81: Entire currently ships two release channels:\n82: \n83: - `stable`: recommended for most users. Stable releases change less often and are the default for Homebrew, Scoop, and `install.sh`.\n84: - `nightly`: prerelease builds for users who want the latest changes earlier. Nightlies are published more frequently and may include newer, less-proven changes than stable.\n85: \n86: How to use each channel:\n87: \n88: - Homebrew stable: `brew install --cask entire`\n89: - Homebrew nightly: `brew install --cask entire@nightly`\n90: - `install.sh` stable: `curl -fsSL https://entire.io/install.sh | bash`\n91: - `install.sh` nightly: `curl -fsSL https://entire.io/install.sh | bash -s -- --channel nightly`\n92: - Scoop: currently supports `stable` only via `scoop install entire/cli`\n93: \n94: ## Typical Workflow\n95: \n96: ### 1. Enable Entire in Your Repository\n97: \n98: ```\n99: entire enable\n100: ```\n101: \n102: On a repo that has not been enabled yet, `entire enable` runs the initial enable flow: it creates Entire settings, installs git hooks, and prompts you to choose which agent hooks to install. To enable a specific agent non-interactively, use `entire enable --agent <name>` (for example, `entire enable --agent cursor`).\n103: \n104: After setup:\n105: \n106: - Use `entire enable` to turn Entire back on if the repo is currently disabled.\n107: - Use `entire agent` to add or remove agents.\n108: - Use `entire configure` to update non-agent settings (telemetry, hooks, checkpoint remote, summary provider).\n109: \n110: The hooks capture session data as you work. Checkpoints are created when you or the agent make a git commit. Your code commits stay clean, Entire never creates commits on your active branch. All session metadata is stored on a separate `entire/checkpoints/v1` branch.\n111: \n112: ### 2. Work with Your AI Agent\n113: \n114: Just use one of your AI agents as before. Entire runs in the background, tracking your session:\n115: \n116: ```\n117: entire status  # Check current session status anytime\n118: ```\n119: \n120: ### 3. Rewind to a Previous Checkpoint\n121: \n122: If you want to undo some changes and go back to an earlier checkpoint:\n123: \n124: ```\n125: entire checkpoint rewind\n126: ```\n127: \n128: This shows all available checkpoints in the current session. Select one to restore your code to that exact state.\n129: \n130: ### 4. Resume a Previous Session\n131: \n132: To restore the latest checkpointed session metadata for a branch:\n133: \n134: ```\n135: entire session resume <branch>\n136: ```\n137: \n138: Entire checks out the branch, restores the latest checkpointed session metadata (one or more sessions), and prints command(s) to continue.\n139: \n140: ### 5. Disable Entire (Optional)\n141: \n142: ```\n143: entire disable\n144: ```\n145: \n146: Removes the git hooks. Your code and commit history remain untouched.\n147: \n148: ## Key Concepts\n149: \n150: ### Sessions\n151: \n152: A **session** represents a complete interaction with your AI agent, from start to finish. Each session captures all prompts, responses, files modified, and timestamps.\n153: \n154: **Session ID format:** `YYYY-MM-DD-<UUID>` (e.g., `2026-01-08-abc123de-f456-7890-abcd-ef1234567890`)\n155: \n156: Sessions are stored separately from your code commits on the `entire/checkpoints/v1` branch.\n157: \n158: ### Checkpoints\n159: \n160: A **checkpoint** is a snapshot within a session that you can rewind to—a \"save point\" in your work.\n161: \n162: Checkpoints are created when you or the agent make a git commit. **Checkpoint IDs** are 12-character hex strings (e.g., `a3b2c4d5e6f7`).\n163: \n164: ### How It Works\n165: \n166: ```\n167: Your Branch                    entire/checkpoints/v1\n168:      │                                  │\n169:      ▼                                  │\n170: [Base Commit]                           │\n171:      │                                  │\n172:      │  ┌─── Agent works ───┐           │\n173:      │  │  Step 1           │           │\n174:      │  │  Step 2           │           │\n175:      │  │  Step 3           │           │\n176:      │  └───────────────────┘           │\n177:      │                                  │\n178:      ▼                                  ▼\n179: [Your Commit] ─────────────────► [Session Metadata]\n180:      │                           (transcript, prompts,\n181:      │                            files touched)\n182:      ▼\n183: ```\n184: \n185: Checkpoints are saved as you work. When you commit, session metadata is permanently stored on the `entire/checkpoints/v1` branch and linked to your commit.\n186: \n187: ### Strategy\n188: \n189: Entire uses a manual-commit strategy that keeps your git history clean:\n190: \n191: - **No commits on your branch** — Entire never creates commits on the active branch\n192: - **Safe on any branch** — works on main, master, and feature branches alike\n193: - **Non-destructive rewind** — restore files from any checkpoint without altering commit history\n194: - **Metadata stored separately** — all session data lives on the `entire/checkpoints/v1` branch\n195: \n196: ### Git Worktrees\n197: \n198: Entire works seamlessly with [git worktrees](https://git-scm.com/docs/git-worktree). Each worktree has independent session tracking, so you can run multiple AI sessions in different worktrees without conflicts.\n199: \n200: ### Concurrent Sessions\n201: \n202: Multiple AI sessions can run on the same commit. If you start a second session while another has uncommitted work, Entire warns you and tracks them separately. Both sessions' checkpoints are preserved and can be rewound independently.\n203: \n204: ## Local Device Auth Testing\n205: \n206: If you're working on the CLI device auth flow against a local `entire.io` checkout:\n207: \n208: ```bash\n209: # In your app repo\n210: cd ../entire.io-1\n211: mise run dev\n212: \n213: # In this repo, point the CLI at the local API\n214: cd ../cli\n215: export ENTIRE_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8787\n216: \n217: # Run the smoke test\n218: ./scripts/local-device-auth-smoke.sh\n219: ```\n220: \n221: Useful commands while developing:\n222: \n223: ```bash\n224: # Run the login flow against a local server (prompts to press Enter before opening the browser)\n225: go run ./cmd/entire login --insecure-http-auth\n226: \n227: # Run the focused integration coverage for login\n228: go test -tags=integration ./cmd/entire/cli/integration_test -run TestLogin\n229: ```\n230: \n231: ## Commands Reference\n232: \n233: | Command          | Description                                                                                       |\n234: | ---------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n235: | `entire clean`   | Clean up session data and orphaned Entire data (use `--all` for repo-wide cleanup)                |\n236: | `entire agent`   | Add, remove, or list agent integrations for the current repository                                |\n237: | `entire configure` | Update non-agent settings (telemetry, git hook, strategy options, summary provider)            |\n238: | `entire disable` | Remove Entire hooks from repository                                                               |\n239: | `entire doctor`  | Fix or clean up stuck sessions                                                                    |\n240: | `entire enable`  | Enable Entire in your repository                                                                  |\n241: | `entire checkpoint`        | List, explain, rewind, and search checkpoints                                           |\n242: | `entire checkpoint explain` | Explain a session, commit, or checkpoint                                               |\n243: | `entire checkpoint rewind` | Rewind to a previous checkpoint                                                         |\n244: | `entire login`   | Authenticate the CLI with Entire device auth                                                      |\n245: | `entire session` | View and manage agent sessions tracked by Entire                                                  |\n246: | `entire session resume`    | Switch to a branch, restore latest checkpointed session metadata, and show command(s) |\n247: | `entire session attach`    | Attach to a previously detached session                                                |\n248: | `entire status`  | Show current session info                                                                         |\n249: | `entire doctor trace` | Show hook performance traces                                                                 |\n250: | `entire version` | Show Entire CLI version                                                                           |\n251: \n252: ### `entire enable` Flags\n253: \n254: | Flag                                        | Description                                                                                                       |\n255: | ------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n256: | `--agent <name>`                            | AI agent to install hooks for: `claude-code`, `codex`, `gemini`, `opencode`, `cursor`, `factoryai-droid`, or `copilot-cli` |\n257: | `--force`, `-f`                             | Force reinstall hooks (removes existing Entire hooks first)                                                       |\n258: | `--local`                                   | Write settings to `settings.local.json` instead of `settings.json`                                                |\n259: | `--project`                                 | Write settings to `settings.json` even if it already exists                                                       |\n260: | `--skip-push-sessions`                      | Disable automatic pushing of session logs on git push                                                             |\n261: | `--checkpoint-remote <provider:owner/repo>` | Push checkpoint branches to a separate repo (e.g., `github:org/checkpoints-repo`)                                 |\n262: | `--telemetry=false`                         | Disable anonymous usage analytics                                                                                 |\n263: \n264: **Examples:**\n265: \n266: ```\n267: # First-time setup with a specific agent\n268: entire enable --agent claude-code\n269: \n270: # Re-enable a disabled repo\n271: entire enable\n272: \n273: # Re-enable and refresh hooks\n274: entire enable --force\n275: \n276: # Save settings locally (not committed to git)\n277: entire enable --local\n278: ```\n279: \n280: `entire enable` is primarily for turning Entire on. On an unconfigured repo it will also bootstrap setup. Use `entire agent` for adding or removing agents, and `entire configure` for non-agent settings.\n281: \n282: ### `entire configure`\n283: \n284: Use `entire configure` to update non-agent settings on a repo that's already set up. Agent installation lives under `entire agent`.\n285: \n286: Typical uses:\n287: \n288: - Toggle telemetry\n289: - Reinstall the Entire git hook (`--force`, `--absolute-git-hook-path`)\n290: - Update strategy options such as `--checkpoint-remote` or `--skip-push-sessions`\n291: - Pick a summary provider for `entire explain --generate`\n292: \n293: **Examples:**\n294: \n295: ```bash\n296: # Show help and the hint pointing to 'entire agent'\n297: entire configure\n298: \n299: # Opt out of telemetry\n300: entire configure --telemetry=false\n301: \n302: # Reinstall the Entire git hook with an absolute binary path\n303: entire configure --absolute-git-hook-path\n304: \n305: # Update strategy options on an existing repo\n306: entire configure --checkpoint-remote github:myorg/checkpoints-private\n307: \n308: # Add or remove an agent\n309: entire agent add claude-code\n310: entire agent remove claude-code\n311: ```\n312: \n313: ## Configuration\n314: \n315: Entire uses two configuration files in the `.entire/` directory:\n316: \n317: ### settings.json (Project Settings)\n318: \n319: Shared across the team, typically committed to git:\n320: \n321: ```json\n322: {\n323:   \"enabled\": true\n324: }\n325: ```\n326: \n327: ### settings.local.json (Local Settings)\n328: \n329: Personal overrides, gitignored by default:\n330: \n331: ```json\n332: {\n333:   \"enabled\": false,\n334:   \"log_level\": \"debug\"\n335: }\n336: ```\n337: \n338: ### Configuration Options\n339: \n340: | Option                               | Values                                       | Description                                             |\n341: | ------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |\n342: | `enabled`                            | `true`, `false`                              | Enable/disable Entire                                   |\n343: | `log_level`                          | `debug`, `info`, `warn`, `error`             | Logging verbosity                                       |\n344: | `strategy_options.push_sessions`     | `true`, `false`                              | Auto-push `entire/checkpoints/v1` branch on git push    |\n345: | `strategy_options.checkpoint_remote` | `{\"provider\": \"github\", \"repo\": \"org/repo\"}` | Push checkpoint branches to a separate repo (see below) |\n346: | `strategy_options.summarize.enabled` | `true`, `false`                              | Auto-generate AI summaries at commit time               |\n347: | `telemetry`                          | `true`, `false`                              | Send anonymous usage statistics to Posthog              |\n348: \n349: ### Agent Hook Configuration\n350: \n351: Each agent stores its hook configuration in its own directory. When you run `entire enable`, hooks are installed in the appropriate location for each selected agent:\n352: \n353: | Agent            | Hook Location                 | Format            |\n354: | ---------------- | ----------------------------- | ----------------- |\n355: | Claude Code      | `.claude/settings.json`       | JSON hooks config |\n356: | Codex            | `.codex/hooks.json`           | JSON hooks config |\n357: | Copilot CLI      | `.github/hooks/entire.json`   | JSON hooks config |\n358: | Cursor           | `.cursor/hooks.json`          | JSON hooks config |\n359: | Factory AI Droid | `.factory/settings.json`      | JSON hooks config |\n360: | Gemini CLI       | `.gemini/settings.json`       | JSON hooks config |\n361: | OpenCode         | `.opencode/plugins/entire.ts` | TypeScript plugin |\n362: \n363: You can enable multiple agents at the same time — each agent's hooks are independent. Entire detects which agents are active by checking for installed hooks, not by a setting in `settings.json`.\n364: \n365: ### Checkpoint Remote\n366: \n367: By default, Entire pushes `entire/checkpoints/v1` to the same remote as your code. If you want to push checkpoint data to a separate repo (e.g., a private repo for public projects), configure `checkpoint_remote` with a structured provider and repo:\n368: \n369: ```json\n370: {\n371:   \"strategy_options\": {\n372:     \"checkpoint_remote\": {\n373:       \"provider\": \"github\",\n374:       \"repo\": \"myorg/checkpoints-private\"\n375:     }\n376:   }\n377: }\n378: ```\n379: \n380: Or via the CLI:\n381: \n382: ```bash\n383: entire enable --checkpoint-remote github:myorg/checkpoints-private\n384: ```\n385: \n386: Entire derives the git URL automatically using the same protocol (SSH or HTTPS) as your push remote. It will:\n387: \n388: - Fetch the checkpoint branch locally if it exists on the remote but not locally (one-time)\n389: - Push `entire/checkpoints/v1` to the checkpoint repo instead of your default push remote\n390: - Skip pushing if a fork is detected (push remote owner differs from checkpoint repo owner)\n391: - If the remote is unreachable, warn and continue without blocking your main push\n392: \n393: #### `ENTIRE_CHECKPOINT_TOKEN`\n394: \n395: `ENTIRE_CHECKPOINT_TOKEN` allows you to provide a dedicated token for checkpoint repository operations, without modifying the credentials used for your primary repository.\n396: \n397: When this environment variable is set, Entire behaves as follows:\n398: \n399: - Injects the token into HTTPS Git operations used for checkpoint fetch and push\n400: - If `checkpoint_remote` is configured:\n401:   - Prefers an HTTPS URL for the checkpoint remote when a token is present, even if the repository’s `origin` uses SSH\n402: - If `checkpoint_remote` is not configured:\n403:   - Falls back to using the default `origin` remote\n404: - If `checkpoint_remote` configuration cannot be loaded:\n405:   - Falls back to `origin`\n406:   - If `origin` is a valid SSH or HTTPS Git remote, Entire converts it to an HTTPS URL to enable token-based authentication\n407: \n408: ### Auto-Summarization\n409: \n410: When enabled, Entire automatically generates AI summaries for checkpoints at commit time. Summaries capture intent, outcome, learnings, friction points, and open items from the session.\n411: \n412: ```json\n413: {\n414:   \"strategy_options\": {\n415:     \"summarize\": {\n416:       \"enabled\": true\n417:     }\n418:   }\n419: }\n420: ```\n421: \n422: **Requirements:**\n423: \n424: - Claude CLI must be installed and authenticated (`claude` command available in PATH)\n425: - Summary generation is non-blocking: failures are logged but don't prevent commits\n426: \n427: **Note:** Currently uses Claude CLI for summary generation. Other AI backends may be supported in future versions.\n428: \n429: ### Settings Priority\n430: \n431: Local settings override project settings field-by-field. When you run `entire status`, it shows both project and local (effective) settings.\n432: \n433: ### Agent-Specific Steps & Limitations\n434: \n435: - When enabling Entire for Codex, the command will also create or update `.codex/config.toml` with `codex_hooks = true` to enable Codex hooks. If you configure Codex manually, make sure this flag is set in your `.codex/config.toml`. Or select Codex from the interactive agent picker when running `entire enable`.\n436: - Entire supports Cursor IDE and Cursor Agent CLI tool, but `entire rewind` is not available at this time. Other commands (`doctor`, `status` etc.) work the same as all other agents.\n437: - Entire supports Copilot CLI, but not Copilot in VS Code, in other IDEs, or on github.com.\n438: \n439: ## Security & Privacy\n440: \n441: **Your session transcripts are stored in your git repository** on the `entire/checkpoints/v1` branch. If your repository is public, this data is visible to anyone.\n442: \n443: Entire automatically redacts detected secrets (API keys, tokens, credentials) when writing to `entire/checkpoints/v1`, but redaction is best-effort. Temporary shadow branches used during a session may contain unredacted data and should not be pushed. See [docs/security-and-privacy.md](docs/security-and-privacy.md) for details.\n444: \n445: ## Troubleshooting\n446: \n447: ### Common Issues\n448: \n449: | Issue                    | Solution                                                |\n450: | ------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------- |\n451: | \"Not a git repository\"   | Navigate to a Git repository first                      |\n452: | \"Entire is disabled\"     | Run `entire enable`                                     |\n453: | \"No rewind points found\" | Work with your configured agent and commit your changes |\n454: | \"shadow branch conflict\" | Run `entire clean --force`                              |\n455: \n456: ### SSH Authentication Errors\n457: \n458: If you see an error like this when running `entire resume`:\n459: \n460: ```\n461: Failed to fetch metadata: failed to fetch entire/checkpoints/v1 from origin: ssh: handshake failed: ssh: unable to authenticate, attempted methods [none publickey], no supported methods remain\n462: ```\n463: \n464: This is a [known issue with go-git's SSH handling](https://github.com/go-git/go-git/issues/411). Fix it by adding GitHub's host keys to your known_hosts file:\n465: \n466: ```\n467: ssh-keyscan -t rsa github.com >> ~/.ssh/known_hosts\n468: ssh-keyscan -t ecdsa github.com >> ~/.ssh/known_hosts\n469: ```\n470: \n471: ### Debug Mode\n472: \n473: ```\n474: # Via environment variable\n475: ENTIRE_LOG_LEVEL=debug entire status\n476: \n477: # Or via settings.local.json\n478: {\n479:   \"log_level\": \"debug\"\n480: }\n481: ```\n482: \n483: ### Cleaning Up State\n484: \n485: ```\n486: # Clean session data for current commit\n487: entire clean --force\n488: \n489: # Clean all orphaned data across the repository\n490: entire clean --all --force\n491: \n492: # Disable and re-enable\n493: entire disable && entire enable --force\n494: ```\n495: \n496: ### Accessibility\n497: \n498: For screen reader users, enable accessible mode:\n499: \n500: ```\n501: export ACCESSIBLE=1\n502: entire enable\n503: ```\n504: \n505: This uses simpler text prompts instead of interactive TUI elements.\n506: \n507: ## Development\n508: \n509: This project uses [mise](https://mise.jdx.dev/) for task automation and dependency management.\n510: \n511: ### Prerequisites\n512: \n513: - [mise](https://mise.jdx.dev/) - Install with `curl https://mise.run | sh`\n514: \n515: ### Getting Started\n516: \n517: ```\n518: # Clone the repository\n519: git clone <repo-url>\n520: cd cli\n521: \n522: # Install dependencies (including Go)\n523: mise install\n524: \n525: # Trust the mise configuration (required on first setup)\n526: mise trust\n527: \n528: # Build the CLI\n529: mise run build\n530: ```\n531: \n532: ### Dev Container\n533: \n534: The repo includes a `.devcontainer/` configuration that installs the system packages used by local development and CI (`git`, `tmux`, `gnome-keyring`, etc) and then bootstraps the repo's `mise` toolchain.\n535: \n536: Open the folder in a Dev Container, or start it from the `devcontainer` CLI as follows:\n537: \n538: ```bash\n539: devcontainer up --workspace-folder .\n540: devcontainer exec --workspace-folder . bash -lc '.devcontainer/run-with-keyring.sh'\n541: ```\n542: \n543: The container's `postCreateCommand` runs `mise trust --yes && mise install`, so Go, `golangci-lint`, `gotestsum`, `shellcheck`, and the canary E2E helper binaries are ready after creation. Use `.devcontainer/run-with-keyring.sh <command>` for commands that touch the Linux keyring, including `mise run test:ci`.\n544: \n545: If `ENTIRE_DEVCONTAINER_KEYRING_PASSWORD` is set in the environment, `.devcontainer/run-with-keyring.sh` uses that value to unlock the keyring non-interactively. If it is unset, the script generates a random password for the session automatically.\n546: \n547: ### Common Tasks\n548: \n549: ```\n550: # Run tests\n551: mise run test\n552: \n553: # Run integration tests\n554: mise run test:integration\n555: \n556: # Run all tests (unit + integration, CI mode)\n557: mise run test:ci\n558: \n559: # Lint the code\n560: mise run lint\n561: \n562: # Format the code\n563: mise run fmt\n564: ```\n565: \n566: ## Getting Help\n567: \n568: ```\n569: entire --help              # General help\n570: entire <command> --help    # Command-specific help\n571: ```\n572: \n573: - **GitHub Issues:** Report bugs or request features at https://github.com/entireio/cli/issues\n574: - **Contributing:** See [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md) for guidelines\n575: \n576: ## License\n577: \n578: MIT License - see [LICENSE](LICENSE) for details.\n\n(End of file - total 578 lines)\n</content>",
